  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We can reflect an object in any line. Consider the diagram below. The two polygons are the mirror images of each other in the line $l.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222660/assets/1e4dfb2939916df5d5018320819f14fcec261a1e77c171b551e9868a8c3bebc8.png)\n\nIf we want to reflect the quadrilateral $ABCD$ below in the line $l,$ we can reflect each vertex separately and then draw the segments between the resulting corresponding vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222660/assets/a47c41b41ecae2bc4c281e1b90c843ae68fa907a0d2ff49c48454fd80e2977c7.png)\n\nLet's start by reflecting the point $A.$ To do this, we need to plot a new point $A'$ on the opposite side of $l$ at an equal distance away from $l$ as the original point $A.$\n\nThe original point $A$ is $4$ units to the left of $l,$ so we need to plot the new point $A'$ at a distance of $4$ units to the right of $l.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222660/assets/33bb82c9d0f20df13f46060b9aec21e8c4d1231766926c47b7718bf58e1c5e32.png)\n\nNow let's reflect other vertices of the quadrilateral in the same way:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222660/assets/00ec763ccce462effa024486a6ff09a98b549c61179de18bb850470e835a7627.png)\n\nFinally, we draw segments between the reflected vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222660/assets/7ffd0cfa45051d8376c46528370b34222a1ebb226987e7530d5081d69942bbde.png)",
